    {
      "id": "the-winters-tale/sceneIII_39.3/p@83406",
      "type": "passage",
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [
        "drama"
      ],
      "text": "Which may if fortune please, both breed thee, pretty,\nAnd still rest thine. The storm begins: poor wretch,\nThat for thy mother’s fault art thus expos’d\nTo loss and what may follow! Weep I cannot,\nBut my heart bleeds, and most accurs’d am I\nTo be by oath enjoin’d to this. Farewell!\nThe day frowns more and more. Thou’rt like to have\nA lullaby too rough. I never saw\nThe heavens so dim by day. A savage clamour!\nWell may I get aboard! This is the chase:\nI am gone for ever.",
      "separatorAfter": "\n\n",
      "spans": [],
      "lines": [
        {
